INSTRCTION : 1. Identify the Document Type: This is a Reinsurance Closing/Offer Slip. Extract key information regarding the accident, policy reference, and financial reserves. 2. Strict JSON Output: Return ONLY a single valid JSON object. 3. Data Formatting: (1) Dates: Convert all dates to `YYYY-MM-DD` format. (2) DateTime: Convert all datetime to `YYYY-MM-DDhh:mm` format (3) Percentages: Convert percentages to decimal or keep as whole numbers depending on context, but ideally as a number (e.g., 20% -> 20.0). 4. Ensure the wording remains exactly as it appears in the source. Do not translate, paraphrase, or modify the original language. 5. Visual Formatting: (1) Use a Bulleted List for multiple insured parties. (2) Use Bold text for the primary entities.
RULES & STRUCTURE : When drafting the slip, you must adhere to the following structural guidelines and industry norms: 1. Clarity & Precision: Use professional insurance terminology (e.g., Cedant, Retention, Layer, Subjectivities). 2/ Standard Layout: Organize the document into a structured list or table format for high scannability. 3. Visual Organization: (1) Apply clear line breaks (newlines) between each paragraph and section to ensure the document is easy to read. (2) Use bullet points for lists of exclusions or conditions. 4. Mandatory Sections: (1) Risk Detail: Type of Reinsurance (Treaty/Facultative), Cedant, and Insured. (2) Period: Inception and Expiry dates (including time zones). (3) Interest: Scope of coverage. (4) Limit & Retention: Gross limit, Ceding company’s retention, and Reinsurance layer. (5) Financials: Premium, Brokerage, and Tax/Levies. (6) Conditions & Exclusions: Specific clauses (e.g., LMA clauses) and standard exclusions. (7) Information: Background on the loss history or risk profile.
OUTPUT : { "Form": Main Policy Form which is The foundational layer within reinsurance documents, treating them as Standardized Policy Wordings and Market Clauses. "Reinsured": The Reinsured (Cedant) name and address without categorization. "Insured": 1. The Policy Holder (who pays the premium) and the Insured (who is covered). 2. All parties listed as having an insurable interest in the project combining all identified clauses into one single text response. "Policy_Holder": 1. Provide the full name exactly. 2. Confirm if the Policy Holder is also listed as an "Insured" party. "Policy_No": 1. Policy Number 2. If no number is found, check if it is marked as "TBA" or "To be advised." "PeriodFrom_c": 1. The Period From (Inception Datetime) information 2. Set 00:01 to `hh:mm` if it does not contain the time information. "PeriodTo_c": 1. The Period To (Expiry Datetime) information 2. Set 00:00 to `hh:mm` if it does not contain the time information. "Location": 1. The Location information from the attached document. 2. Provide the complete physical address of the insured project. 3. Mention the specific project site or building name associated with this location. 4. Confirm the country and province to determine the legal and regulatory territory. "Territory_Scope": 1. The Territory Scope information. 2. Provide the ISO 2-letter code for the identified territory 3. If the Territory Scope is `Worldwide`, state `WW` 4. If there is no value, state `null` "Choice_of_Law_and_Jurisdiction": 1. Choice of Law and Jurisdiction 2. Provide the ISO 2-letter code for the identified territory 3. If there is no value, state `null` "Occupancy_Business": 1. "Occupancy" or "Nature of Business" to assess the technical risk profile of the insured interest. 2. Identify the general industry. 3. Provide the full description of the work or business being conducted at the location. 4. Mention if the occupancy is currently in the "Construction phase," "Testing phase," or "Operational phase" based on the policy period and clauses. "Covered_Risk": 1. Covered Risk 2. Identify the primary section and its value. 3. State the name of the standard form used 4. Provide a list of all clauses that provide extra coverage along with any specific limits mentioned. "Interest": 1. Interest 2. Provide the full description of the project or property being insured. 3. List the specific insurance sections associated with this interest. 4. Briefly mention if the interest includes additional items like "Inland Transit" or "Existing Property" based on the clauses. "Gross_Prem_100": 1. Gross Premium 2. Identify the total premium for the all layers. "Gross_Prem_DP_Applied": 1. Gross Premium and Deposit Premium. "RI_Cession_Reins_Percent": 1. RI Cession (Reins Percent) (e.g,. not 19% but 19) "RI_Comm_Percent": 1. Reinsurance Commission (e.g,. not 19% but 19) "Other_Deduction": 1. Please analyze the document and provide: (1) Are there any mentions of Brokerage or Taxes being deducted ? (2) Does the calculation (Gross * Share) - Comm = Net hold true? 2. Specific Clauses: Check the "Terms & Conditions" for any clauses mentioning "Taxes" or "Premium Payment Warranty" that might imply future deductions. 3. Provide the entire result as a single continuous string. "Limit_of_Liability": 1. Limit of Liability 2. Provide the full layer structure. 3. Combine the keys and values of both sections into one continuous text block. 4. State the maximum amount the entire layer covers. 5. Identify the percentage share of the reinsurer. 6. Calculate the exact monetary amount the reinsurer is liable for in a total loss scenario. "Deductible": 1. Identify the base deductible 2.List deductibles for specific risks like Windstorm, Flood, Earthquake, and Testing. 3. Check if there is any clauses that defines how deductibles are applied to consecutive natural disasters. "Terms_and_Conditions": 1. Terms and Conditions 2. Identify all clauses and items starting from the "Terms and Conditions" or "Clauses" section. 3. Combine all identified clauses into one single text response. 4. Ensure each specific clause or numbered item starts on a new line (using a line break/carriage return). 5. Do not summarize, paraphrase, or omit any clause. Keep the original numbering and the full technical wording. "Extension_Clauses": 1. identify all "Extension Clauses" that broaden the scope of the standard policy wording to assess the full breadth of risk. 2. Combine all identified clauses into one single text response. 3. Ensure each specific clause or numbered item starts on a new line (using a line break/carriage return). 4. Do not summarize, paraphrase, or omit any clause. Keep the original numbering and the full technical wording. "Additional_Information": 1. Capture all "Supplementary Data" and "Contextual Notes" that are not part of the standard financial or period fields but are crucial for contract management. 2. Note any technical details about the project (e.g., testing phases, specific materials). 3. Identify the contact persons or departments listed for the Reinsured and Reinsurer. 4. List any comments or notes found in the footer or the very last page that don't fit into standard categories. "ProportionalReinsurance": Identify the sharing ratios and financial distribution in a Proportional Reinsurance treaty. [ "CurrencyVal" : Convert any currency symbols or names into their official ISO 4217 three-letter codes. "GrossPremium" : - Gross Premium - Number without comma and without non-numerical values "Reins" : Reinsurance Share (e.g,. not 19% but 19) "ReComm" : Reinsurance Commission (e.g,. not 19% but 19) "NetReInsPremium" : Net Reinsurance Premium (e.g,. not 19% but 19) ] "Total_Sum_Insured": 1. The Total Sum Insured can be found on several sections. 2. Perform a mathematical summation of all identified amounts to calculate all numeric values for the Total Sum Insured "Total_Sum_Insured_Currency": 1. Convert any currency symbols or names into their official ISO 4217 three-letter codes. "TSIbyLocation" : Total Sum Insured (TSI) by Location [ "TSI": 1. Total Sum Insured for the section. "TSI_Currency": 1. Total Sum Insured Currency for the section. 2. Convert any currency symbols or names into their official ISO 4217 three-letter codes. ] "LocationbyTSI" : Physical location for the Location by TSI [ "Location" : The detail address for the location ] }

